# Line endings belong to the file, not to the machine that cloned it.
#
# There was no `.gitattributes` here until 2026-10-08, and `core.autocrlf=true`
# is set on the machine this repository is developed on. A clean checkout
# therefore wrote `tools/*.sh` with CRLF — verified by cloning the repository and
# reading the bytes, not reasoned about.
#
# That matters because bash does not tolerate it: with CRLF, every line of a
# script carries a trailing CR, so the first command bash reads is the whole line
# plus a stray carriage return. The failure has nothing to do with the script.
#
# `actions/checkout` does not touch `core.autocrlf` — checked by searching its
# source, where the string does not appear — so what a runner receives is
# whatever that machine's git is configured to do. This file is the only thing
# that makes a checkout deterministic.
#
# Only shell scripts are listed. Everything else survives either ending: YAML
# workflows are parsed server-side from the committed blob, Rust and JSON do not
# care, and Python and Node read their sources through parsers that do not
# either. The rule is here for the files where it is load-bearing.
*.sh text eol=lf
